window.DM_BENCHP={"meta":{"last":"2026-11-10","n_all":1256,"built":"2026-11-11","note":"Each Dream Menu figure is set beside published research on the same topic: national polls, trade bodies, wholesalers, sales data and menu trackers. Outside figures are quoted with their source and date and never mixed into Dream Menu data. Where the two differ, the card explains the local reason."},"items":[{"id":"pineapple","topic":"Pineapple on pizza","theme":"Debates","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"level","dm":{"v":49.3,"unit":"%","text":"49.3% say yes","base":"1,230 guests"},"ext":{"v":53,"unit":"%","text":"53% of Britons like pineapple on pizza","who":"YouGov, GB adults","date":"2017","url":"https://yougov.co.uk/news/2017/03/06/does-pineapple-belong-pizza"},"extra":"A January 2024 YouGov survey again found that more than half of Britons love or like it (reported by Reuters, January 2025).","read":"Foodies split almost exactly like the general public. Pineapple is a genuine 50:50 call, so it belongs on a pizza menu as an option, not a statement."},{"id":"tea","topic":"Tea or coffee","theme":"Debates","tier":"public","verdict":"differs","kind":"level","dm":{"v":67.4,"unit":"%","text":"67.4% pick coffee (25.4% tea)","base":"957 guests"},"ext":{"v":37,"unit":"%","text":"Among under-35s, 37% prefer coffee and 25% prefer tea","who":"YouGov polling, reported by Beverage Daily","date":"July 2025","url":"https://www.beveragedaily.com/Article/2025/07/01/delifrance-brews-up-fresh-insight-into-the-uks-coffee-craze/"},"extra":"Across all British adults, 41% drink tea and 40% drink coffee at least twice a day (YouGov Profiles, 2025). In 2012, 52% of Britons preferred tea.","read":"Even against younger Britons, the most coffee-led group in national polling, Dream Menu guests lean much further to coffee. That reflects who answers (urban, food-led, many in the trade), so read coffee demand here as the leading edge, not the national average."},{"id":"irnbru","topic":"Irn-Bru or Coke","theme":"Debates","tier":"public","verdict":"differs","kind":"level","dm":{"v":46.1,"unit":"%","text":"46.1% pick Irn-Bru (33.4% Coke)","base":"1,229 guests"},"ext":{"v":21,"unit":"%","text":"Irn-Bru was 21% of 330ml cans sold with Scottish takeaways, against 36% for Coke","who":"Scoffable takeaway orders, Scotland","date":"2022","url":"https://www.scoffable.com/article/irnbru-vs-coke-ten-years-of-takeaway-data"},"extra":"AG Barr says Irn-Bru accounts for one in every two flavoured fizzy drink cans in Scotland and grew ahead of the soft-drinks market (Circana, 12 weeks to 18 July 2026).","read":"Irn-Bru wins the loyalty question; Coke wins the order that comes with a takeaway meal. Both are true: one is identity, the other is habit. A range in Scotland needs both, with Irn-Bru as the local signal."},{"id":"water","topic":"Tap water with dinner","theme":"Debates","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"level","dm":{"v":34.7,"unit":"%","text":"34.7% ask for tap water","base":"1,230 guests"},"ext":{"v":33,"unit":"%","text":"About a third of people drink tap water when eating out in restaurants","who":"Local Government Association survey, 3,000 people","date":"2016","url":"https://www.yorkshirepost.co.uk/health/tap-water-should-be-offered-families-eating-out-1799806"},"read":"Roughly one diner in three takes tap water in both sources. The rest split between still and sparkling, so a bottled water offer still matters to two tables in three."},{"id":"matcha","topic":"Matcha","theme":"Trends","tier":"public","verdict":"differs","kind":"direction","dm":{"dir":"down","text":"The trend guests most want binned, named by 9.2% of all interviews","base":"1,256 interviews"},"ext":{"dir":"up","text":"Consumers choosing matcha lattes 33.5% more than a year earlier; matcha dishes on UK menus doubled","who":"Tastewise, UK consumer and menu data","date":"June 2026","url":"https://tastewise.io/blog/uk-menu-trends"},"extra":"A matcha iced latte was one of the UK’s top trending Deliveroo orders of 2025.","read":"Growth and backlash at once: the classic sign of a polarising trend near its peak. Serve it well for the fans who want it, but don’t build a menu identity around it."},{"id":"pistachio","topic":"Dubai chocolate and pistachio","theme":"Trends","tier":"public","verdict":"differs","kind":"direction","dm":{"dir":"down","text":"Second most-named trend to bin, named by 8.4% of all interviews","base":"1,256 interviews"},"ext":{"dir":"up","text":"Pistachio reaching consumers 44% faster than a year earlier; pistachio with chocolate up 28%","who":"Tastewise, UK consumer data","date":"June 2026","url":"https://tastewise.io/blog/uk-menu-trends"},"read":"The same pattern as matcha. Pistachio as a flavour still has room; the viral Dubai bar format is what foodies are tired of."},{"id":"loaded","topic":"Loaded and dirty food","theme":"Trends","tier":"public","verdict":"differs","kind":"direction","dm":{"dir":"down","text":"3.0% of all interviews name loaded and dirty food as a trend to bin","base":"1,256 interviews"},"ext":{"dir":"up","text":"Loaded fries and shareable potato platters recommended as plant-forward wins for pubs and bars","who":"UKHospitality trend report","date":"2026","url":"https://www.ukhospitality.org.uk/?p=10357"},"read":"A gap between what the trade is advised to add and what foodies say they are tired of. Worth testing before adding loaded formats to a menu."},{"id":"tiramisu","topic":"Tiramisu","theme":"Dishes","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"direction","dm":{"dir":"flat","text":"Steady staple: 12.2% of dessert answers, between 10.3% and 14.5% in every full quarter","base":"dessert answers"},"ext":{"dir":"up","text":"Tiramisu up nearly 15% in consumer demand","who":"Tastewise, UK consumer data","date":"June 2026","url":"https://tastewise.io/blog/uk-menu-trends"},"read":"The biggest single dessert on the Dream Menu, and still growing in the wider market. As safe as dessert gets."},{"id":"ooh","topic":"How often Scotland eats out","theme":"Market","tier":"public","verdict":"context","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"Dream Menu asks what people would choose, not how often they buy","base":"1,256 interviews"},"ext":{"text":"Out-of-home transactions in Scotland fell 7% in 2025, to 809 million; delivery transactions fell 30%","who":"Food Standards Scotland, Worldpanel by Numerator","date":"2026","url":"https://www.foodstandards.gov.scot/science-and-evidence/monitoring-out-of-home-food-and-drink-purchases-in-scotland-and-great-britain-2024-2025"},"read":"Fewer trips make each visit count for more. Dream Menu shows what people want from the visits they still make: the dishes worth leaving the house for."},{"id":"sites","topic":"Venues trading","theme":"Market","tier":"public","verdict":"context","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"Guests have named 1,695 venues","base":"venue mentions"},"ext":{"text":"Britain had 98,319 licensed venues in September 2026, 977 fewer than a year earlier (−1.0%)","who":"NIQ Hospitality Market Monitor (CGA data)","date":"October 2026","url":"https://www.vinetur.com/en/20261030110108/britain-lost-977-licensed-hospitality-venues-in-the-year-to-september.html"},"read":"A shrinking market concentrates demand. The venues guests name repeatedly are the ones with a reason to visit that survives a squeeze."},{"id":"italian","topic":"Italian food","theme":"Dishes","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"Italian ranks on starters, mains and desserts: 8.0% of starters, 8.6% of mains, and tiramisu is the most-named dessert (12.2%)","base":"course answers"},"ext":{"text":"Italian is Britain’s favourite cuisine, chosen by 51% (Chinese 46%, British 43%, Indian 41%)","who":"YouGov, GB adults","date":"April 2022","url":"https://yougov.com/en-gb/articles/41659-part-three-favourite-cuisines-and-junk-food"},"read":"The national favourite shows up across the whole dream menu, not just at the pizzeria. Glasgow’s Italian heritage makes it even stronger here: a safe anchor for any range or menu."},{"id":"stp","topic":"Sticky toffee pudding","theme":"Dishes","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"The number 2 dessert, 10.9% of dessert answers","base":"dessert answers"},"ext":{"text":"73% of Britons who have tried sticky toffee pudding like it, one of the best-liked British desserts","who":"YouGov, classic British dishes ranked","date":"June 2019","url":"https://yougov.com/en-gb/articles/23758-classic-british-cuisine-ranked-britons"},"read":"Widely liked by the public and a dream dessert for foodies: the rare dish that is both a crowd-pleaser and an aspiration. Yet only 23% of the dessert menus we audited list it."},{"id":"pizza","topic":"Takeaway pizza","theme":"Debates","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"The most-named guilty pleasure, named by 6.6% of all interviews","base":"1,256 interviews"},"ext":{"text":"Takeaway pizza is one of Britain’s three favourite junk foods, picked by 31% (41 to 42% of under-40s)","who":"YouGov, GB adults","date":"April 2022","url":"https://yougov.com/en-gb/articles/41659-part-three-favourite-cuisines-and-junk-food"},"read":"Foodies and the public share the same comfort food. Pizza is the treat people return to, which is why it sells on delivery even when it is nobody’s dream main."},{"id":"diet","topic":"Diet and zero-sugar cola","theme":"Drinks","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"Diet Coke and Coke Zero are named by 10.2% of soft drink answers, against 3.6% for full-sugar Coca-Cola","base":"soft drink answers"},"ext":{"text":"More than seven in ten soft drinks sold in the UK are low- or no-calorie","who":"British Soft Drinks Association, Annual Industry Insights Report","date":"July 2026","url":"https://www.beveragedaily.com/Article/2026/07/22/uk-soft-drinks-saw-record-value-in-2025-despite-global-challenges/"},"read":"The same shift in both: sugar-free is the default cola now. A soft drinks range should lead with zero-sugar, with full-sugar as the option."},{"id":"margarita","topic":"Margaritas","theme":"Drinks","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"Margarita and spicy margarita together take 10.7% of alcoholic drink answers, the biggest single cocktail family","base":"alcoholic drink answers"},"ext":{"text":"The Margarita is number one in the top 10 cocktails for 2026","who":"Bacardi Cocktail Trends Report, via Morning Advertiser","date":"December 2025","url":"https://www.morningadvertiser.co.uk/Article/2025/12/19/key-cocktail-trends-for-pubs-in-2026"},"extra":"Dream Menu adds the twist: the spicy version is named more often than the classic.","read":"Both put the margarita first. The local detail is heat: guests want the spicy one, so a menu with only a classic margarita is missing the version they ask for."},{"id":"martini","topic":"Martini","theme":"Drinks","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"5.3% of alcoholic drink answers, the number 3 cocktail","base":"alcoholic drink answers"},"ext":{"text":"The Dry Martini is in the top 10 cocktails for 2026","who":"Bacardi Cocktail Trends Report, via Morning Advertiser","date":"December 2025","url":"https://www.morningadvertiser.co.uk/Article/2025/12/19/key-cocktail-trends-for-pubs-in-2026"},"read":"The martini revival the trade reports is visible in what foodies name: a classic that earns its place on a short list."},{"id":"nolo","topic":"Alcohol-free","theme":"Drinks","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"7.9% answer the alcoholic drink question with something alcohol-free","base":"151 alcoholic drink answers since August 2026"},"ext":{"text":"44% of UK adults choose no- or low-alcohol drinks to moderate (49% of young adults), up from 31% in 2018","who":"Drinkaware Monitor","date":"October 2025","url":"https://www.drinkaware.co.uk/news/half-of-young-adults-are-now-choosing-no-low-alcohol-drinks"},"read":"Moderation is mainstream. Dream Menu shows how far it goes: about one guest in 13 picks alcohol-free even when asked for their dream alcoholic drink. Give it its own priced section."},{"id":"wom","topic":"Word of mouth","theme":"Places","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"The 10% most-named venues take 44% of all venue mentions","base":"venue mentions"},"ext":{"text":"59% of Gen Z discover restaurants through recommendations from friends or family; 43% through social media","who":"YouGov, Gen Z eating out","date":"August 2026","url":"https://yougov.com/en-gb/articles/55399-how-gen-z-consumers-eat-out-in-2026-attitudes-restaurant-types-and-discovery-drivers"},"read":"Recommendation is still the main way people choose, and it concentrates: a small set of venues gets most of the word of mouth. Being the place people name matters more than being seen."},{"id":"smallplates","topic":"Small plates","theme":"Trends","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"direction","dm":{"dir":"down","text":"One of the trends guests most want binned, named by 2.9% of all interviews","base":"1,256 interviews"},"ext":{"dir":"down","text":"56% of UK diners now prefer individual dishes to sharing plates","who":"OpenTable, reported by The Independent","date":"March 2026","url":"https://www.aol.com/news/no-longer-radical-tyranny-small-060000356.html"},"read":"Both point the same way: small-plate fatigue is real. Guests want a proper main they don’t have to share or order three of."},{"id":"scallops","topic":"Scottish seafood","theme":"Dishes","tier":"public","verdict":"agrees","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"Seafood is 35.8% of dream starters; scallops are the most-named starter (7.8%)","base":"starter answers"},"ext":{"text":"Scallops are the most favoured Scottish seafood species in UK Michelin-starred restaurants; 80% of starred restaurants use Scottish seafood","who":"Seafood Scotland, Making Waves","date":"April 2025","url":"https://www.seafoodscotland.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/SFS-Making-Waves-Report-2025_Web.pdf"},"read":"Chefs at the top of the guides and guests choosing a dream starter land on the same thing: Scottish scallops. The strongest local story in the data."},{"id":"steakchicken","topic":"Steak against chicken","theme":"Dishes","tier":"public","verdict":"differs","kind":"context","dm":{"text":"Steak and beef are 25.5% of dream mains; chicken 5.6%","base":"main answers"},"ext":{"text":"Chicken is 62.4% of the meat-centred meals Britons eat out of home; beef grew 2.7%","who":"AHDB, Worldpanel by Numerator","date":"October 2025","url":"https://ahdb.org.uk/news/consumers-craving-meaty-meals-when-dining-out"},"read":"Chicken is what Britain eats out; steak is what it dreams of. Guests trade up to beef for the meals that matter, which is where a premium steak range earns its margin."}]};
